<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludwig Erhard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludwig Erhard was a German economist and politician of the Christian Democratic Union who, as director of economics in the western zones, ended price controls alongside the currency reform of 1948. As economics minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> from 1949 to 1963 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a> he promoted the social market economy and is credited with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">Wirtschaftswunder</a>. He succeeded Adenauer as chancellor from 1963 to 1966.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludwig-erhard.jpg" alt="Ludwig Erhard" width="320" height="487" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Koch for Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Einde_bezoek_bondskanselier_dr_Ludwig_Erhard_en_gaf_persconferentie_in_het_Haag,_Bestanddeelnr_916-1330.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
